hniques.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-22T09:18:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39754153727", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Abū ʿAbdallāh Yaʿīsh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Yūsuf ibn Simāk al-Andalusī al-Umawī was a 15th-century Spanish-Arab mathematician.", "newValue": "Yaʿīsh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Umawī, a distinguished 15th-century Spanish-Arab mathematician, stands as a pivotal figure in the transmission and advancement of arithmetic, particularly in the realm of fractions and root extraction. Active in Granada during the twilight years of Nasrid rule, al-Umawī's work represents a sophisticated culmination of Islamic mathematical traditions, synthesizing insights from earlier scholars like al-Uqlīdisī and al-Kāshī. His most celebrated contribution, a unique method for computing the square root of fractions, demonstrated a remarkable algorithmic ingenuity that would resonate with subsequent generations of mathematicians.

Al-Umawī's seminal treatise, the *Kitāb al-tarīqah al-wāḍiḥah fī ʿilm al-misāḥah* ('The Clear Method in the Science of Mensuration'), though primarily focused on practical geometry, contained extensive sections on arithmetic, specifically addressing operations with sexagesimal and decimal fractions. He developed a novel technique for extracting the square root of fractional numbers that involved converting them into a specific form to simplify the calculation, circumventing the complex direct methods then prevalent. This methodological clarity and precision were characteristic of the advanced mathematical culture flourishing in Al-Andalus even as its political fortunes waned.

The legacy of Yaʿīsh al-Umawī lies in his role as a conduit for sophisticated mathematical concepts from the Islamic world to Europe, particularly through the Iberian Peninsula. His methods, alongside those of other Andalusian scholars, became part of the intellectual corpus that influenced early Renaissance mathematicians, providing practical solutions for commerce, astronomy, and engineering. The clarity and rigor of his work helped to standardize fractional arithmetic, making complex calculations more accessible and paving the way for further algebraic developments."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-22T09:18:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39754153727", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1400, al-Andalus"}, {"title": "Accumulation of mathematical knowledge and formal systems", "type": "Idea", "year": "1400, al-Andalus"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing of advanced mathematical scholarship within Al-Andalus created a fertile intellectual environment for Yaʿīsh al-Umawī's innovations.", "The practical demands of commerce, astronomy, and land surveying during the late medieval period necessitated sophisticated arithmetic for fractions and roots.", "The extensive preservation and development of Greek and Indian mathematical traditions by Arab scholars provided the foundational knowledge upon which Yaʿīsh al-Umawī built his work."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-22T09:18:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39754153727", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Advanced mathematical knowledge used by subsequent scholars", "type": "Idea", "year": "1489"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1489"}], "newValue": ["Yaʿīsh al-Umawī's unique methods for fractional arithmetic significantly advanced computat